{"slides":[{"k":"title","h":"WHAT is happening?","sub":"Session 2 · Live class · Spend, suppliers and contracts as business insight","note":"Welcome everyone back and check sound and cameras. Remind them: in Session 1 we chose WHO matters; today we prepare WHAT we bring to them. They have watched the lessons, so today is not a lecture: it is about using the ideas and arguing about them. Ask everyone to keep their workbook and data pack open."},{"k":"table","h":"Today's plan","t":[["Time","Block","How"],["09:00","Fieldwork debrief","Poll and chat"],["09:30","2.1 Spend analytics and Nordvia data lab","Debate and breakout rooms"],["10:30","Break",""],["10:40","2.2 From numbers to insight","Live ladder in the chat"],["11:05","2.3 Supplier and contract landscape","Positioning debate"],["11:45","Break",""],["11:55","2.4 Presenting data to stakeholders","Build your Spend Story"],["12:35","Peer challenge: the 2-minute CIO test","Breakout trios"],["12:55","Close and Fieldwork 2",""]],"note":"Walk through the plan in under a minute. Point out the two breaks so people can plan. Say the output of the day is their own five slide titles, tested on a peer playing the stakeholder. Ask them to mute when not speaking, but to use the chat freely at any time."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s2-l1","note":"Launch the poll straight away as an energiser: 60 seconds. Read the result aloud without judging. If many have not held the interview, do not shame anyone: say today's work gives them a better reason to book it. Use the result to pick two or three volunteers for the next slide."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Fieldwork debrief: what did they tell you?","q":"Name one thing your stakeholder said they do not know about their own spend.","b":["One goal they are measured on this year","One project coming in the next 18 months","One pain point with a supplier","The question you did not dare to ask"],"min":20,"format":"Chat waterfall, then open floor","note":"Ask everyone to type their answer in the chat but press Enter only on your count of three, so nobody copies. Read out the patterns: unknown totals, licences, consultants. Invite two or three people to tell their story in one minute each. Capture recurring projects and frustrations on the whiteboard: they will feed the Spend Story later. Keep it to 20 minutes."},{"k":"section","n":"2.1","h":"Spend analytics essentials","sub":"Who spends what, with whom, under which contract?","note":"Short bridge: the lesson covered the spend cube and the eight analyses. We will not repeat them. We will test whether they hold up on real-looking data, and argue about what the Group numbers mean for how we work."},{"k":"bars","h":"Our Group: two very different games","d":[["Below €50K",86,"86% of suppliers · 12% of spend"],["€50K–250K",10,"10% · 25%"],["€250K–1M",3,"3% · 34%"],["Above €1M",1,"1% · 29%"]],"note":"Let the chart sit for a few seconds, then say the message: about 800 relationships to negotiate professionally and about 5,000 small ones to simplify. Figures are rounded and anonymised. Ask: how much of your week goes to the 5,000? Take two or three answers, then move to the provocation on the next slide."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Should we stop managing small suppliers?","q":"\"Procurement should stop touching any supplier below €50K a year.\"","b":["What do we gain in time and focus?","What risk do we accept: compliance, fraud, data?","Who would take over: catalogues, cards, the business?","Where would you set the line, and why?"],"min":10,"format":"Agree / disagree","note":"Ask people to react with a thumbs up (agree) or thumbs down (disagree), then call one person from each side. Push both sides: the agree side must say how risk is still controlled; the disagree side must say what they would drop instead. Land the point: different suppliers need different processes, not the same effort. Ten minutes maximum."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s2-l2","note":"Run the poll in 45 seconds. Most groups land on 'mostly' or 'mess'. Remind them of the coding errors from the lesson: travel agencies booked as transportation, rent as facility management, meal vouchers as consulting. Key line: a stakeholder will forgive a missing analysis, never a wrong number. Clean first, then analyse."},{"k":"table","h":"The Nordvia IT data pack","t":[["Supplier","Service","€M","Contract","Expiry"],["Northbyte","Infrastructure outsourcing","19.4","Yes","Dec 2027"],["Kelvar","Core licences","11.2","Yes","Jun 2027"],["Orbis","Devices and support","7.8","Yes","Mar 2027"],["Brightline","IT consulting","6.1","No","n/a"],["Telmo","Network and telecom","4.9","Yes","Extended"],["305 others","Various","32.6","Partly","Various"]],"note":"Remind them Nordvia is a fictional insurer and the suppliers are invented. Give them 30 seconds to look at the table in silence. Ask one quick question to the room: what is the first thing you notice? Do not give the answers yet: that is the data lab."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Nordvia data lab","min":35,"where":"Breakout rooms of 3–4 · Module 2.1 in the workbook","b":["Answer the four check questions from the data pack","Agree which supplier worries the CIO most","Run four of the eight analyses on your own data","Note one analysis your data cannot support"],"note":"Open breakout rooms of three or four for 35 minutes; post the brief in the chat before opening them. Visit each room for a minute or two; the check answers are validated automatically in the workbook. Send a five-minute warning. Expected answers: €82M total, 60% with the five named suppliers, €19M expiring in 12 months, €6.1M Brightline without contract."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s2-l3","note":"Run the poll as soon as everyone is back from the rooms. Do not reveal your own view. The split is the point: it shows that 'worry' depends on whether you look at money, risk, regulation or time. Use the result to set up the debate on the next slide."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Northbyte or Brightline: what is the real problem?","q":"\"The biggest supplier is never the biggest risk.\"","b":["Northbyte: critical ICT provider, in the DORA register","Brightline: €6.1M from 12 call-offs, no contract","Telmo: rolled over twice, nobody looked","Which one would the CIO want to hear about first?"],"min":10,"format":"Open floor","note":"Ask one room to defend Northbyte and another to defend Brightline, one minute each. Then open the floor. Steer towards the idea that there is no single right answer: it depends on the stakeholder's goals, which is why the fieldwork interview matters. Close the module: clean data, eight analyses, two games. Then announce the break."},{"k":"statement","h":"Break · 10 minutes","p":"Back at 10:40. Think: which number from your own data would surprise your stakeholder most?","note":"Put a visible timer on screen. Leave the question on the slide so people come back with an answer. Use the break to read the chat and the whiteboard notes from the debrief. Restart on time, even if a few people are still missing."},{"k":"section","n":"2.2","h":"From numbers to insight","sub":"Stakeholders do not want data. They want to know what it means for them.","note":"Restart by asking two people for their 'surprising number' from the break question. Then introduce the module: we climb from fact to question. The lesson showed the ladder; now we practise it live together."},{"k":"steps","h":"The insight ladder","s":[["Fact","58% of IT spend is under contract"],["Insight","€34M is bought without agreed prices or exit clauses"],["Implication","Your run-cost target is exposed; two suppliers are critical"],["Question","Which of these services will you still need in 2027?"]],"note":"Walk up the ladder one step at a time, slowly. Stress the implication step: it must be in the stakeholder's words, about their goals. 'Contract coverage' means nothing to a CMO; 'money spent without an agreed price' does. Two minutes, then go straight to the live exercise."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Climb this fact, live","q":"\"The Nordvia media buying contract expired eight months ago.\" Take it to the top of the ladder.","b":["Insight: why does it matter? (€18M a year)","Implication: what does it mean for the CMO?","Question: what would you ask her?","Vote: which answer sounds most like a CMO?"],"min":15,"format":"Chat waterfall","note":"Ask for the insight first in the chat, then the implication, then the question, about two minutes each. Read three answers per step aloud and ask the room which one a CMO would care about. Challenge any answer that slips into Procurement language. Ask: at which step do we usually stop in real life? Most will admit: at the fact."},{"k":"bars","h":"Case 2: IT grew €10M. Where did it go?","d":[["Cloud pilot (new scope)",4.1,"+€4.1M"],["Brightline (unmanaged volume)",2.3,"+€2.3M"],["Tail and other",1.7,"+€1.7M"],["Devices for new hires (volume)",1,"+€1.0M"],["Kelvar uplift (price)",0.9,"+€0.9M"]],"note":"This is the case bridge from €72M to €82M. Ask the room: which of these were business decisions and which were costs nobody managed? Expected split: cloud and devices were decided; Kelvar and Brightline were not managed. Point out that 'spend went up' starts a blame game, while a bridge starts a conversation. Five minutes, then move on to 2.3."},{"k":"section","n":"2.3","h":"Supplier and contract landscape","sub":"Which suppliers matter, how risky are they, and what expires soon?","note":"Bridge: 2.1 told us where the money is. Now we add risk and time. Two tools: the portfolio matrix and the renewal calendar. Both are simple; the debate is in the details."},{"k":"cards","h":"Four positions, four strategies","c":[["Leverage","High spend, many alternatives: compete and renegotiate"],["Strategic","High spend, few alternatives: partner, manage, plan the exit"],["Bottleneck","Low spend, hard to replace: secure supply"],["Routine","Low spend, easy to replace: simplify"]],"note":"One-minute recap of the Kraljic matrix: financial impact on one axis, supply risk on the other. Remind them that in insurance, regulation such as DORA partly writes the risk axis. Ask: which quadrant takes most of your time today? Usually Routine, which ties back to the two games."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Where does Kelvar belong?","q":"\"Kelvar's core licences are Leverage: software is a commodity, so tender it.\"","b":["How long would switching core licences take?","What does the 9% uplift tell us?","Does the position change if we look at the whole Group?","Place Orbis, Brightline and Telmo too"],"min":15,"format":"Agree / disagree, then open floor","note":"Ask for a quick show of hands or reactions on the statement. Many will agree at first; push back: switching core licences takes years, so Kelvar is Strategic, managed with a usage review and a benchmark. Then ask them to place Orbis, Brightline and Telmo in the chat: expected answers are Leverage for all three. Point out that positions move over time: a migration can turn a Strategic supplier into Leverage."},{"k":"table","h":"Nordvia IT renewal calendar","t":[["Contract","€M","Expiry","Start work by"],["Telmo","4.9","Extended","Now"],["Orbis","7.8","Mar 2027","Now: Group framework"],["Kelvar","11.2","Jun 2027","Now: notice Dec 2026"],["Northbyte","19.4","Dec 2027","Already late"]],"note":"Say it plainly: today is October 2026 in the case, and everything on this calendar is already urgent. Recall the lead times: 12 to 18 months for complex outsourcing, 9 to 12 for a tender, 4 to 6 for a renegotiation. Notice periods are illustrative. Ask: how would the CIO react if she heard this six months from now?"},{"k":"discuss","h":"Whose job is the renewal calendar?","q":"\"Contract renewals are the business owner's job. Procurement just runs the tender when asked.\"","b":["Who actually sees every expiry date today?","What did the Telmo roll-overs cost?","60–70% of next year's work comes from expiring contracts","What would the CIO lose if we waited to be asked?"],"min":10,"format":"Agree / disagree","note":"Expect a mixed room: some teams really do work this way. Let one defender speak first, then ask the others for counter-examples from their own entities. Land the point: the calendar is the cheapest way to be ahead of the stakeholder, and it shows we know their business. Then announce the second break."},{"k":"statement","h":"Break · 10 minutes","p":"Back at 11:55. Bring your own top five suppliers and their expiry dates.","note":"Put the timer on screen. The prompt prepares the Spend Story work: they need their own data ready. Check in privately with anyone who was quiet in the debates, if there is time. Restart on time."},{"k":"section","n":"2.4","h":"Presenting data to stakeholders","sub":"One message at a time, in their units, about their goals.","note":"Restart with energy: this is where everything comes together. Say: if they need you in the room to understand the slide, the slide has failed, because leaders forward slides. The goal of this block is five titles that work without you."},{"k":"two","h":"Topic titles versus finding titles","l":{"t":"Topic","b":["Contract overview","Supplier analysis","Tail spend"]},"r":{"t":"Finding","b":["Two renewals worth €19M fall in nine months","Five suppliers take 60% of your IT budget","140 suppliers cost more to manage than they bill"]},"hi":"r","note":"Read the left column, then the right one, and let the contrast speak. Explain the rule: the finding goes in the title, not the topic. Ask them to keep asking 'so what?' until the title says something. Then launch the poll."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s2-l4","note":"Run it in 30 seconds; nearly everyone picks 'Five suppliers take 60%'. Then ask the harder question: so why do most Procurement decks still use topic titles? Take one or two honest answers, such as templates, habit, fear of being wrong. Keep it short."},{"k":"steps","h":"The 5-slide Spend Story","s":[["At a glance","Total, trend, share of company spend"],["Where it goes","Top services and suppliers"],["What is coming","Renewals and known projects"],["What we see","Three risks, three opportunities, value ranges"],["What we suggest next","Questions and a working session"]],"note":"Walk through the five slides quickly: they saw the Nordvia examples in the lesson. Stress slide 5: it ends with questions and a proposed next step, not a savings target. Mention the one-page Spend Fact Sheet as the alternative format for the playback meeting."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Do savings belong in the Spend Story?","q":"\"Never show a savings number in a first meeting with a stakeholder.\"","b":["What does the stakeholder hear when we say 'savings'?","Value range or no number at all?","Who owns the number: them or us?","What did the CFO in Case 2 actually want to know?"],"min":10,"format":"Pairs in breakout rooms, then report back","note":"Send people to pairs for four minutes, then ask three pairs to give their verdict in one sentence. A good outcome is nuance: show value ranges tied to their goals, never a Procurement target. Link back to Case 2: the CFO wanted an explanation, not a promise. Six minutes of plenary at most."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Your Spend Story and the 2-minute CIO test","min":40,"where":"Workbook Module 2.4, then breakout trios","b":["20 min alone: write your five finding titles","Trios: present in two minutes","Peer 1 plays the stakeholder and interrupts","Peer 2 notes every piece of jargon","Rotate so everyone presents once"],"note":"First 20 minutes are individual in the main room, cameras optional; then open trios for 20 minutes. Post the roles in the chat before opening the rooms. Observers check three things: was the message clear in 30 seconds, in the stakeholder's language, and did it end with a question? Send warnings at 13 and 6 minutes so every rotation happens."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Debrief: what did the CIO test reveal?","q":"Which of your titles survived the 'so what?' and which died?","b":["The jargon your peer caught most often","One title you rewrote on the spot","The question you would end the meeting with"],"min":10,"format":"Chat waterfall, then open floor","note":"Ask everyone to paste their best title in the chat on your count. Read five aloud and ask the room to vote with reactions for the one they would read first. Invite two people to share a title that died and how they fixed it. Keep it light and fast: this is the final energy peak before the close."},{"k":"steps","h":"Fieldwork 2, before Session 3","s":[["Playback","30 minutes with your stakeholder: show the Spend Fact Sheet"],["Ask","What surprises you? What is wrong? What is coming?"],["Pipeline","List every project, renewal and change, with timing, value and owner"],["Correct","Fix your data with what they told you"]],"note":"Brief the fieldwork clearly: the playback meeting is short on purpose. They show that Procurement knows the spend and wants the stakeholder's view; they do not propose a strategy yet. The demand pipeline is the input for Session 3. Ask everyone to put the meeting date in the chat as a commitment."},{"k":"takeaways","h":"Session 2 in five lines","b":["One wrong number costs the meeting: clean first","800 relationships to manage, 5,000 to simplify","Climb from fact to a question, in their words","Position by impact and risk; the calendar is already urgent","Finding titles, five slides, end with a question"],"note":"Read the five lines slowly, then ask each person to type in the chat the one line they will use first. Thank them for the debate. Announce Session 3: WHAT should we propose? They should bring their corrected data and their demand pipeline. Finish on time at 13:00."}],"polls":[{"id":"s2-l1","q":"How did your discovery interview go?","options":["Held it, it went well","Held it, it was hard","Booked, not yet held","Not booked yet"]},{"id":"s2-l2","q":"Would you show your stakeholder's spend data to them tomorrow, as it is?","options":["Yes, I trust it","Mostly, after some fixes","No, coding is a mess","I have not looked yet"]},{"id":"s2-l3","q":"Which Nordvia IT supplier should worry the CIO most?","options":["Northbyte: critical outsourcing","Kelvar: 9% licence uplift","Orbis: Group framework is cheaper","Brightline: €6.1M without contract","Telmo: rolled over twice"]},{"id":"s2-l4","q":"Which slide title would your stakeholder read first?","options":["IT spend analysis 2025","Five suppliers take 60% of your IT budget","Procurement savings opportunities","Supplier landscape overview"]}]}